    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i have a \"Products\" table where \"SupplierID\" is the key, and \"SupplierName\" and \"SupplierPhone\" are both in there, they're technically dependent on \"SupplierID\"... but \"SupplierName\" is also dependent on \"SupplierID\" through \"SupplierID\" itself? i'm a bit fuzzy on the terminology. can you give me a concrete example of a transitive dependency that i should look out for in a messy table?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A transitive dependency in this case means that \"SupplierName\" and \"SupplierPhone\" both depend on \"SupplierID\", but \"SupplierName\" directly depends on \"SupplierID\", not on the value stored in \"SupplierName\" or \"SupplierPhone\".\n\nHere's an example:\n\nSuppose your \"Products\" table has columns for \"ProductID\", \"ProductDesc\", \"QuantityInStock\", \"SupplierID\", \"SupplierName\", and \"SupplierPhone\". The transitive dependency here is between \"QuantityInStock\" and \"SupplierName\", because \"QuantityInStock\" depends on the supplier's current stock levels, which are determined by storing the supplier's name. This is not a dependency on \"ProductID\", since the product can exist without knowing the supplier's name.\n\nYou should aim to remove \"SupplierName\" from the Products table, and create a new \"SupplierDetails\" table that includes both \"SupplierID\" and \"SupplierName\" (or related information). This would help eliminate the transitive dependency and ensure data normalization.",
      "source": "target",
